  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b581008b6ecbab147b4e8289eb5868c9, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  startLettersTexts:
  - content: Dear Daddy,
  - content: Dear Mary,
  - content: Dear sister,
  - content: Dear Mr Jones,
  - content: Dear father,
  - content: Dearest Lea,
  - content: 'Darling, '
  bodyLettersTexts:
  - content: "I hope you are not alarmed, you should not be. Unlike the past few
      months, these days the front is calm, the weather is nice and we have food
      and water. Since William\u2019s unexpected death, I suffer from crippling fear.
      They told us that we will have a small truce this summer for Mary\u2019s Heavenly
      Ascension. I really just want to come back home and hug you all."
  - content: "Life on the Eastern Front is safe, but worrying. We are currently not
      fighting because we are out of resources, but we move back each day to flee
      from the Russian army and I fear they are moving forward our borders. Peter
      has some spare coffee; we drink it together to ease our nerves. Whenever I
      drink something warm while looking at the sunset, it always reminds me of us
      just a few years ago. I miss those days before the war; now I can\u2019t go
      a single day without worrying that if we fail, we will bring the enemy's army
      to our doorstep. I can\u2019t wait to see you again."
  - content: "Thank you for your letter. I am happy to hear that you and Mum are
      well, despite everything. You even remembered to water my rosemary; I hope
      its scent can remind you a little bit of me. If I make it back I will thank
      you for taking care of my things. A few days ago, during a retreat, we ran
      into a minefield. I don\u2019t even have to tell you that it was not marked.
      It was a massacre: ten of us didn\u2019t make it out alive and the other five
      were injured because of an explosion. I\u2019ve been treated and I\u2019m feeling
      better now. Sergeant Hyam told us that it won\u2019t happen again and that
      we are safe now\u2026 I hope he is right. I love you and tell Mum I can\u2019t
      wait to see her again."
  - content: Thanks awfully for your letter. I am glad Professor Parker could continue
      his research without the help of his students. I am just sad that I could not
      continue my academic career because of this war. Professor Parker's company
      was charming with all the knowledge he could exchange so easily yet so wisely;
      compared to that, the individual who goes by the name of Corporal Giles is
      an obnoxious presence. We spent the last few weeks walking through French territory
      to come back home. We are all tired because we do not eat nor sleep enough;
      all that unbearable corporal does is scream in the faces of the privates who
      have the misfortune of not keeping pace. I just want to go back to my studies
      and my old life.
  - content: 'I imagine you are wondering why I have not written, but it was a difficult
      time. I am truly grateful for your kind words; I needed to hear something like
      that. We have been under fire for three months now and we are in awful conditions
      compared to when we departed. I heard rumors that at the other front, where
      Daniel is, they are doing a little bit better than us; they are retreating,
      but at least they are alive. Here we have lost almost everyone: there were
      nearly three hundred defending this position and now we are way less than two
      hundred. About a half. I do not think we will hold out much longer. This may
      be my last letter to you.'
  - content: "I am writing to you in despair. I do not know which information is
      coming from the front but we truly had no chance against the enemy\u2019s army.
      I hope this letter will reach you before them. You have to leave the country
      right now. I am out of danger and I will reach a safe place soon. Just do as
      I said and we will reunite after the war."
  - content: Yesterday I had to visit all the units again, to gather statements for
      my monthly report. Every time I checked, we had lost a sheer amount of soldiers.
      Nevertheless, we did not have enough ammunition and food to move somewhere.
      They really had low morale, but I did not expect something like this; as soon
      as I arrived at the camp, everyone had gone, even the officers! Deserters!
      I will be home in a few days. I am done with my job here. I wish you and our
      kids all the best. We will hug soon.
  endLettersTexts:
  - content: 'Yours, Albert '
  - content: 'Forever yours, J. '
  - content: 'Take care, Liam '
  - content: 'A big hug, Tom '
  - content: 'Love you, Sam '
  - content: 'I miss you, Andy '
  - content: 'Yours, Mark '
  endFirstLetterWord:
  - war;
  - signaled.
  - presence.
  - alive.
